From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 08:53:37 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat(llama-cpp): context on demand - a model keeps its
 weights, a request gives its context back

A loaded llama.cpp model holds its weights and its context (the KV cache and
the compute buffers), the second whether or not a request is running. With
the `context_on_demand` model option the backend keeps the weights and lets a
request give the context back: a request that carries `keep_context_ms` in
its `metadata` has the context freed that many milliseconds after its answer
(0: at once), and the next request creates it again - requests back to back
carry a keep that bridges the time between them. A request that carries none
uses the context as it always did: it is not freed after it. `keep_context_ms`
as a model option gives a keep to the requests that carry none. Several
models on one card then need room only for the contexts that work at the same
time.

- patches/0011-server-context-on-demand.patch: the llama.cpp side
  (--context-on-demand, --keep-context-ms, the request's keep_context_ms),
  written for upstream with its server tests and README.
- context_on_demand.h (+ test): the keep parsed from the request's metadata
  and from the model option, and what a task's result says of its context;
  the fields are detected, so the source still builds against pins and
  forks without the feature.
- grpc-server.cpp: Predict, PredictStream and Embedding give their tasks the
  request's keep; the model options `context_on_demand` and
  `keep_context_ms`. No RPC reads the llama_context any more - parse_options,
  TokenizeString and Detokenize take the vocab, Embedding's layout the
  pooling type read once at the load - because with the option the context
  comes and goes on the server's loop thread and is null while the model is
  idle.
- Embedding says beside its answer whether the context was created for that
  call: the trailing metadata `llama-context-created-us` carries the
  microseconds the creation took, or -1 when the context was already there
  (only for a model loaded with the option). It is the server's own word
  per call, for a caller that counts creations - one cannot be told from
  the caller's clock, since the context may have been kept for another
  caller's request. No change to backend.proto.
- the context canary: llama.cpp holds every context it creates to the
  output the load's context gave for a fixed input, before any request runs
  on it, and refuses the request of one that does not reproduce it (see the
  llama.cpp patch). It is on with `context_on_demand`; the model option
  `context_canary:false` switches it off. Embedding says a canary failure
  beside its error (trailing metadata `llama-context-canary: failed`), so a
  caller can tell a discarded context from memory that could not be had.

Checked on the CPU (grpc-server built from this tree, random-weight qwen3 and
llama models, driven over gRPC as LocalAI drives it): the load's context is
freed when idle; TokenizeString and Detokenize answer while idle and create
none; one creation per Embedding with a keep of 0, the same vector across
creations; a keep in the metadata holds the context and a later keep of 0
does not shorten it; token ids are embedded on demand too; a request without
a keep leaves the context loaded, and so does a keep that is no number, until
a request that carries one is answered; without the option nothing is ever
freed and the vector is the same, byte for byte; the model option gives a
keep to a request without one; a creation made to fail is an error on that
request, the backend stays up and serves the next; Predict gives the same
completion across creations; an Embedding answer says the microseconds of
the creation made for it and -1 when the context was there, as many times as
the server logged a creation, and says nothing without the option; the
canary recorded at the load and reproduced by every context created; a
context made to fail it discarded, the call refused INTERNAL with the
trailer, the backend up and the next call served with the same vector; a
creation refused for memory carrying no such trailer; `context_canary:false`
recording and checking nothing.

